t  whether its perspective getting on its  own  or if that story about abdi is combined  with the information  and again we don't see any interaction  here where combining the two makes them  even more effective  the third outcome has to do with policy  preferences  um so we tell them every year the  federal government decides how many  refugees to allow to be resettled in the  united states  and we then give them some context so  they know what those numbers have looked  like and how much they've bounced around  we say this year the number of refugees  permitted to enter is 125 000 next year  do you think the number should be higher  lower  or stay the same  and so again information here providing  that correction on vetting  doesn't  doesn't make people more supportive of  admitting more refugees if anything  um there's actually a slight backlash  here although that is not statistically  significant  um however again giving the narrative  about abdi does seem to make people more  willing to say they would want to  increase the number of refugees admitted  that's significant and substantively  fairly meaningful  and again combining the two  doesn't really do much difference as we  thought it might  it's slightly higher  for the combination but that difference  is not significant  and then finally we ask would you be  willing to write a letter to the current  president's administration  advocating for your position on how many  refugees should be admitted into the  united states so they could say yes they  would like to write a letter supporting  an increase  yes they would like to write a letter  opposing an increase or no they would  not like to write a letter and if they  said yes in support or opposition  we ask them to actually write the letter  to make this as behavioral as possible  and we said um and we will send it  anonymously to the administration on  their behalf after completion of the  survey  so this is what the results look like  here and again they're quite consistent  where um correcting on vetting if  anything has a slight backlash although  again this is not significant um so  you're not seeing that spillover  however the perspective getting  treatments on their own and in  combination with the information  do  increase willingness to write a  supportive letter for raising the  refugee  cap so to summarize these main effects  of the treatments um we do find that  misconceptions on security threat can be  corrected um whether that's information  just on its own blandly provided or  whether that's embedded in a perspective  getting exercise  however that information on its own  doesn't really seem to improve attitudes  and behaviors relative towards the  control and recall here that we were  trying to pick a piece of information  where clearly people often have had it  wrong and where also it's it seems  theoretically and also from some of the  regression analysis we did that this  should be  related um to attitudes towards refugees  so we were trying to make this as strong  as possible  but still  it didn't really work beyond just  correcting the misconception that being  said of course it may well be that  another piece of information would have  been more effective and maybe we just  chose wrong  on the other hand we do find that  perspective getting on its own or with  information  really does improve attitudes and  behaviors at least in the context of the  survey um  making respondents more inclusive in how  they approach refugees as people and  also policies  however this doesn't actually cause them  to update their beliefs about the  vetting process  which is not necessarily surprising  so now i want to talk about  the heterogeneous treatment effects i  had mentioned  um in terms of whether that perspective  getting treatment specifically  is more or less likely to be effective  based on dispositional empathy  and so again it could be that those with  more empathy actually respond less well  to this treatment or that those with  more empathy respond better  because they're more capable of  processing the out group's perspective  and in fact what we see  for the three outcomes where the  perspective getting mattered um was that  these effects were much stronger among  individuals who had higher levels of  baseline empathy so in other words  people who are just generally better at  empathizing with others um  we were more likely to increase their  inclusive views in response to the  treatment than individuals who um just  for whatever reason are less empathetic  so this is what it looks like with the  feeling thermometer  um  among individuals who are below average  in their baseline empathy the feeling  the perspective getting exercise  with abdi increased the average response  on the thermometer by about four  percentage points  however among those um with a higher  level of baseline empathy  the average on the thermometer almost  doubled  the treatment effect almost doubled um  to eight percentage points so  uh this difference is significant and  fairly large  uh  so  scott are these um  results sort of um  do do they  are they the same by gender or by other  characteristics of uh  they say  are they still  so obviously find the same level of  positive for example you would find that  one is  more than steel  so i believe we've looked with  partisanship and it  doesn't seem to  differ consistently um  and  likewise i'm not sure about gender but  that would be a good one for us to look  at  um there definitely may be  other heterogeneous effects here that  we want to look at  but this pattern does hold with controls  included so  um  it seems you know it seems like as as  well as we can do since we're not  randomly assigning levels of empathy  um that this pattern does persist  um  and i guess for me partly it's quite  strong because it's consistent across  those three outcomes so this is what it  looks like for  the policy outcome where  you actually see a slightly  negative effect for those low in empathy  for the combined treatment although not  significant  but you see quite large effects and  positive effects  um for those high in empathy  and then again also with letter writing  there's a slight boost for those low and  baseline empathy but  um there's a fairly large boost for  those higher in baseline empathy so  you know when when we're doing this  analysis it may be that there's some um  other omitted variable explaining the  heterogeneous effects  we can't rule that out but it seems like  on average those with  a higher capacity to empathize with  others in general are the ones who are  most likely to be  affected by treatments that try to prime  empathy and i do think logically that  makes sense um  and and isn't so surprising so  practically that might suggest that  these are the kinds of people to target  with these  exercises when you're designing  messaging  so  just to start wrapping up in terms of  implications um  there is some debate over whether  trying to  reduce prejudice generally or change  attitudes on refugees and immigration is  better served by doing this sort of  information correction  or by trying to leverage empathy about  specific people and their stories and  and our study  is not the first to show but builds on  others suggesting that these empathy  generating treatments  are more effective um  typically than trying to correct  people's  stereotypes or falsely held beliefs  but again those effects seem to be  stronger for  those with high baseline empathy and in  fact  they may  not exist at all for those who are lower  on average and empathizing with others  at the same time we do show that  correcting misconceptions is possible so  when we told people that  the us government does all this  extensive betting there wasn't that much  resistance to them  agreeing with that information is and  updating their priors and saying okay  yeah um i had it wrong this is how much  vetting they actually go through so to  the extent that we care about correcting  misinformation um  even if we're not showing in this study  that that has spillover effects in other  ways that we might care about it does  still seem that it is possible to get  people to update on these sorts of  issues even when they're closely tied to  pretty touchy political topics and  debates  and  to us the main takeaway here is that  interventions combining perspective  getting more perspective taking  along with information correcting  misconceptions may be the most effective  at addressing a range of outcomes  because even though we didn't find an  interaction as we were expecting where  these treatments became more effective  by combining them together  the combined treatment was the one that  corrected people's misconceptions and  also had these effects on prejudice  through the perspective getting  narrative um so this may have practical  implications for messaging that's  emphasized by advocates  if you're going to provide information  and updates  uh try to update people's priors you  might want to work in these humanizing  stories along with it um  if your goal ultimately is to increase  support for admitting refugees  alongside correcting those  misconceptions likewise  if you're going to humanize  refugees as part of a prejudice  reduction strategy then you might also  try and work in information  make the most of addressing as many  outcomes as possible  in terms of where we go next um so  we would like to think more about  heterogeneous effects and do some  exploratory analysis in thinking about  who these interventions work best for  uh we're only showing this short term in  the survey so it may be that these  effects dissipate pretty quickly in fact  they probably do  uh so we may try to look at persistence  and then potentially we'd be interested  in trying to scale this up into  an experiment experiment with actual  messaging in the field so that's  potentially another step also 